    "id": "thm:rectangle-frontier",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "For every compatible \\(p_x\\), \\(v_x^{\\Box}(p_x)=v_x(p_x)\\) if and only if \\(\\mathcal F_x^{\\mathrm{val}}(p_x)\\neq\\varnothing\\).  Moreover, simultaneous preservation of the value and complete optimizer correspondence,\n\\[\n v_x^{\\Box}(p_x)=v_x(p_x)\\quad\\text{and}\\quad Q_x^{\\Box}(p_x)=Q_x^*(p_x),\n\\]\nholds if and only if \\(\\mathcal F_x^{\\mathrm{arg}}(p_x)\\) holds.  Both conditions admit finite observable basis certificates.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "p_x",
      "v_x^{\\Box}(p_x)",
      "v_x(p_x)",
      "\\mathcal F_x^{\\mathrm{val}}(p_x)",
      "Q_x^{\\Box}(p_x)",
      "Q_x^*(p_x)",
      "\\mathcal F_x^{\\mathrm{arg}}(p_x)"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:rectangle",
      "def:rectangle-frontier",
      "lem:joint-saddle-background"
    ],
    "justification": "Value preservation and rule preservation are distinct questions, and the theorem locates their exact observable frontiers.",
    "gap": "HruzaEtAl2026CategoricalPIL uses coordinatewise bounds in its loss, while SongEtAl2026CategoricalIV supplies joint restrictions; no prior theorem states when discarding those restrictions is decision-neutral.",
    "consumer": "The certificate tells a user of interval-based categorical policy learning whether the existing objective is safe or must be replaced by the joint oracle.",
    "proof_tex": "Let \\(f(q)=h_{\\mathcal M_x(p_x)}^{\\max}(q)=\\max_bh_{\\mathcal M_x(p_x)}(e_b-q)\\) and define \\(g(q)\\) analogously with \\(\\mathcal M_x^{\\Box}(p_x)\\).  The coordinate extrema defining the rectangle imply \\(\\mathcal M_x(p_x)\\subseteq\\mathcal M_x^{\\Box}(p_x)\\), so support-function monotonicity gives\n\\[\n f(q)\\leq g(q)\\quad(q\\in\\Delta_K),\\quad v_x(p_x)\\leq v_x^{\\Box}(p_x).\n\\]\nIf \\(q\\in\\mathcal F_x^{\\mathrm{val}}(p_x)\\), then \\(g(q)\\leq v_x(p_x)\\), whence\n\\[\n v_x(p_x)\\leq v_x^{\\Box}(p_x)\\leq g(q)\\leq v_x(p_x).\n\\]\nThus the values agree.  Conversely, if the values agree, compactness supplies \\(q^{\\Box}\\in Q_x^{\\Box}(p_x)\\), and \\(g(q^{\\Box})=v_x(p_x)\\), so \\(q^{\\Box}\\in\\mathcal F_x^{\\mathrm{val}}(p_x)\\).\n\nNow suppose \\(\\mathcal F_x^{\\mathrm{arg}}(p_x)\\) holds.  For \\(q\\in Q_x^*(p_x)\\),\n\\[\n v_x(p_x)=f(q)\\leq g(q)\\leq v_x(p_x),\n\\]\nso \\(q\\in Q_x^{\\Box}(p_x)\\), and the preceding value argument gives \\(v_x^{\\Box}=v_x\\).  Conversely, for \\(q\\in Q_x^{\\Box}(p_x)\\),\n\\[\n v_x(p_x)\\leq f(q)\\leq g(q)=v_x^{\\Box}(p_x)=v_x(p_x),\n\\]\nso \\(q\\in Q_x^*(p_x)\\).  Hence both correspondences agree.  If instead simultaneous value and correspondence preservation is assumed, every \\(q\\in Q_x^*=Q_x^{\\Box}\\) satisfies \\(g(q)=v_x^{\\Box}=v_x\\), which is exactly \\(Q_x^*\\subseteq\\mathcal F_x^{\\mathrm{val}}\\).\n\nFinally, both \\(f\\) and \\(g\\) have finite epigraph descriptions.  Basis enumeration for \\(f\\), endpoint enumeration for \\(g\\), and finite primal--dual feasibility checks therefore give the claimed observable certificates, including weak inequalities on boundary faces.",
